Build fix, macOS: pull-to-refresh needs an AppKit header Apple does not ship.

HAVE(NSREFRESHCONTROLLER) is switched on for every build that targets
macOS 27. What it enables, WKWebView+RefreshControl.swift, conforms
WKWebView to AppKit.NSRefreshControlHosting, a protocol that is in Apple's
internal SDK and not in the one that comes with Xcode. With the public SDK
the WebKit Swift module does not compile, and nothing that depends on it
does either.

Switch the feature on only when the private header it is built from is
there, which is the test AppKitSPI.h already makes. A build with the public
SDK is then without pull-to-refresh in the view, which is a gesture of the
host application and not a feature of the web.

--- a/Source/WTF/wtf/PlatformHave.h
+++ b/Source/WTF/wtf/PlatformHave.h
@@ -1940,7 +1940,8 @@
 #endif
 
 #if !defined(HAVE_NSREFRESHCONTROLLER) \
-    && ((PLATFORM(MAC) && __MAC_OS_X_VERSION_MIN_REQUIRED >= 270000))
+    && ((PLATFORM(MAC) && __MAC_OS_X_VERSION_MIN_REQUIRED >= 270000)) \
+    && __has_include(<AppKit/NSRefreshControl_Private.h>)
 #define HAVE_NSREFRESHCONTROLLER 1
 #endif
 
